{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And as for the people, he removed them to cities.</b> Rashi explained: to remove the disgrace from his brothers, so that people would not say, 'They are exiles.' But this is far-fetched, that for this small benefit Joseph would uproot the entire land. Furthermore, one is not called an exile and displaced unless he had an inheritance of field or vineyard and was driven from them; but Jacob had not been a resident in any place at all, and all the patriarchs were sojourners — for sojourning had already begun from the birth of Isaac, and we do not find that the patriarchs were concerned about this. And it is more fitting to explain all this upheaval in light of what is said (Deuteronomy 10:19): ״וַאֲהַבְתֶּם אֶת הַגֵּר, כִּי גֵרִים הֱיִיתֶם בְּאֶרֶץ מִצְרָיִם״ — 'and you shall love the stranger, for you were strangers in the land of Egypt.' For anyone who was never himself a stranger does not feel the pain of the stranger and has no pity on the stranger's life; but one who was himself a stranger knows the pain of the stranger, and whatever is hateful to himself he does not do to his fellow. Therefore Joseph moved the people to cities so that they would all be strangers in a land not their own, in order that they might feel the pain of the stranger. And it could be that Rashi also intended this, for this is the disgrace with which they would shame them, saying: 'You are exiles! — and if HaShem desired your good, why are you exiles? And if so, it is permitted to do evil to you.' Therefore Joseph moved them to cities so that they could reply to them: 'You too have been made sick as we have' (after Isaiah 14:10).'" "Another matter: Joseph moved them to cities for this reason — for he saw that Jacob and his sons had settled in the land of Goshen and had taken hold of it as a possession and inheritance in the land, and there was reason to fear that after many days a tyrant would arise and would demand from the book of the chronicles of every city and its inhabitants, in which was recorded the memorial of every tract of land — to whom it belonged — and he would find for Israel no claim that their fathers had held inheritances there, and not one of them could argue, 'This land belonged to my fathers,' and he would drive them from it. Therefore he moved all of them to cities, so that not one of them dwelt on his fathers' inheritance, and Israel would be like any one of them — for all of them would be dwelling without legal title."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {0 "The Aramaic maxim כָּל דַּעֲלֵיהּ סְנֵי לְחַבְרֵיהּ לָא יַעֲבֵיד ('whatever is hateful to yourself, do not to your fellow') is rendered literally into English in keeping with the voice rules."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:33:57.077021405Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}